        {
          "id": 50620794,
          "author": "icyfox",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T23:09:16.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50620794",
          "textZhHtml": "Some takes (and from talking to nvidia people):<p>- Delaware precedent in Caremark has a very high bar for culpability of boards in matters like this. So I suspect the combination of DoJ saying they won't prosecute combined with this standard will successfully remove their individual liability.</p><p>- I'm surprised this decision was completely unanimous at the board level, considering how they quickly forced him out. Full 180 in under two weeks.</p><p>- The news media is really confusing selling chips to Megaspeed then hosting them in Malaysia (giving chinese companies access - afaik still legal under the old export regime) and Megaspeed smuggling these physical chips for hosting in Chinese data centers. Former was legal and second was not.</p><p>- I would not be surprised if a pardon was mentioned at some point. Will be watching how state AGs end up getting involved. CA has already subpoenaed Nvidia for this.</p><p>- There was a drum circle at Nvidia from employees who were petitioning to get him back</p><p>Disclosure: I co-host a podcast that talks about this. More discussion if anyone is interested: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t8nLiELIhwA\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t8nLiELIhwA</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50623117,
          "author": "etempleton",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T03:09:11.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50623117",
          "textZhHtml": "The reporting around this was pretty sloppy / showed a lack of understanding.<p>Most news reports would have you believe that this all happened yesterday when in fact it happened years ago. They also seemed to  immediately believe that Jensen Huang was proven complicit in chip smuggling to China, which, as far as I am aware of, there is no proof of or really any insinuation of from legitimate sources. And then they would raise concerns about circular financing, which I agree is longer term a potential issue, but everyone jumped straight to Enron levels of financial manipulation, which I find a farfetched.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50633171,
          "author": "ksec",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T19:24:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50633171",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;The news media is really confusing selling chips<p>&gt;The reporting around this was pretty sloppy / showed a lack of understanding.</p><p>I love the optimism these top two comment shows. If only it was true, they would and should have backtracked when they have learned and understand their mistakes.</p><p>Not only have they not. They have doubled down.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50624459,
          "author": "hn_throwaway_99",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T06:24:06.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50624459",
          "textZhHtml": "All of what you wrote feels like a bunch of strawmen designed to minimize Huang's significant misconduct:<p>&gt; Most news reports would have you believe that this all happened yesterday</p><p>I literally don't know what you're talking about given that there were a ton of news reports that highlighted the US government was aware of Huang's affair in the early part of this year. A big part of this story was the US govt was aware of the affair for a long time and Trump himself was notified back in May.</p><p>&gt; They also seemed to immediately believe that Jensen Huang was proven complicit in chip smuggling to China, which, as far as I am aware of, there is no proof of or really any insinuation of from legitimate sources.</p><p>Again, perhaps some Twitter-type news sources said that, but the mainstream media sources never made that link.</p><p>What is so enraging about Huang being reinstated is it is yet another case of \"in your face oligarchy\" with \"rules for thee, but not for me\". His conduct (having an affair with one of Nvidia's biggest customers whose primary business was making Nvidia compute available to China, having an affair with a direct report, having an affair with someone else at the company, and now it's being reported by WSJ he admitted to <i>additional</i> affairs at the Sunday board meeting) would get you fired from any company I can think of. I saw a spot-on joke that the employee handbook at Nvidia has been revised to \"do whatever the fuck you want as long as you make money\". The only reason he was reinstated is due to pressure from Trump because he wanted the stock market to go up.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50633628,
          "author": "hardbass",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T19:52:08.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50633628",
          "textZhHtml": "So where is the crime here? Sleeping around is not a crime. If he runs the company well and his absence is made known as unpleasant by the board, share price, employees etc, who cares. Complain about nvidia having made such a successful product or the company wanting him back, not the \"political system\"."
        },
        {
          "id": 50636344,
          "author": "otterley",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T23:30:38.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50636344",
          "textZhHtml": "I want you as well as anyone else who has this question to do a basic news search to answer it. The allegations of criminal activity relating to violations of trade sanctions are easy to find.<p>Here’s a Google or AI agent phrase you can use: “what crime is Jensen Huang suspected of committing as CEO of Nvidia?”  See? Easy.</p><p>Please, before you make comments, at least <i>try</i> to see if there’s anything out there that might contradict your own narrative. Otherwise you just look like a god damned fool who I’d barely trust to make a sandwich, let alone engineer anything.</p>"
        },
        {
