      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50576609,
          "author": "djoldman",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T15:54:31.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50576609",
          "textZhHtml": "These proofs really are quite huge. The proof is 1.3GB, ~15M lines of code:<p></p><pre><code>  mkdir -p riemann_proof &amp;&amp; cd riemann_proof\n\n  # fetch parts 01–14\n  for i in {01..14}; do\n    curl -fSL -o lean.tar.gz.part$i https://alpo.ge/lean.tar.gz.part$i || { echo \"failed: part$i\"; break; }\n  done\n\n  # join and decompress (creates ./lean)\n  cat lean.tar.gz.part{01..14} &gt; lean.tar.gz\n  tar xzf lean.tar.gz\n\n  # size, file count, line counts\n  du -sh lean | cut -f1 | xargs printf '%s on disk (lean folder)\\n'\n  find lean -name '*.lean' | wc -l | xargs printf '%s .lean files\\n'\n  find lean -name '*.lean' -print0 | xargs -0 cat | wc -l | xargs printf '%s lines total\\n'\n  find lean -name '*.lean' -print0 | xargs -0 cat | grep -cv '^[[:space:]]*$' | xargs printf '%s non-blank lines\\n'</code></pre>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50580197,
          "author": "hafeoz",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T19:10:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50580197",
          "textZhHtml": "Finally finished compiling on my PC (well, actually an HPC machine at my company, but it's the weekend, and I hope nobody is using it) - it took 2.4 hours on a 480-core Xeon and produced 89 GB of compilation output. I had to make some modifications because, for some reason, Lake bottlenecks and stalls verification. I worked around this by compiling the files directly instead of using `lake build`. I also had to increase `vm.max_map_count` for some later modules.<p>All 175,187 modules seem to check out with Lean 4.33.1, with no errors or `sorry`. There are tons of linter warnings - over 460k of them - but I think they might be caused by the way I invoked it. I haven't checked the actual axiom being proved, though - I'm not good at math :)</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50585062,
          "author": "jychang",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T01:54:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50585062",
          "textZhHtml": "175,187 modules... a lot of them are repetitive.<p>It really seems he was rushed to publish and did not bother cleaning the code up at all. I am compiling the code and reviewing it, and there are 204 dead modules that are not referenced anywhere. And a LOT of repetitive code.</p><p>I think this should not be 17 million lines, I bet this can easily be under 1 million lines of Lean with a little bit of cleanup.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50587051,
          "author": "fspeech",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T08:04:14.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50587051",
          "textZhHtml": "It is probably not that compressible. Anthropic FLT proof also has a lot of redundancies but my projection is without true mathematical refactoring it will be hard to get to 50% reduction in line count. One problem is that Lean, or type theory really, is truly verbose. In these parallely developed proofs if two agents use slightly incompatible definitions there are a lot of translations back and forth. Also FLT is following routes well established by mathematicians and it still got to 13 million lines. So I am actually surprised that GRH is not much bigger, as it reportedly ranges over a wide swath of fields."
        },
        {
          "id": 50587500,
          "author": "joachimma",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T09:24:38.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50587500",
          "textZhHtml": "Anthropic claims to have a 1/7 the size version somewhere.\n<a href=\"https://claude.dev/blog/safely-scaling-multi-agent-teams-with-fable-5-2/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://claude.dev/blog/safely-scaling-multi-agent-teams-wit...</a>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50590969,
          "author": "fspeech",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T16:42:29.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50590969",
          "textZhHtml": "Yes it would be interesting to see that version. If compaction comes from proof architectural improvement (what I  called \"true mathematical refactoring\"), it could improve readability. On the other hand if it is tactical, for example using tall rw stacks that are incomprehensible to humans, shortness doesn't necessarily imply better accessibility. I suppose it has to be a lot of the first kind to get that much compression."
        },
        {
          "id": 50586132,
          "author": "daveads5",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T05:08:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50586132",
          "textZhHtml": "Even with all the resources he presumably have… interesting"
        },
        {
          "id": 50586908,
          "author": "aylmao",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T07:32:26.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50586908",
          "textZhHtml": "Hence why it seems rushed. Backed by Anthropic he certainly can clean it, model access or $$$ is probably not a limiting factor; only time.<p>I don't know if Anthropic IPO being yesterday, or American Mathematical Society Executive Committee and Board of Trustees meeting being today has anything to do with the announcement happening today, but it's certainly interesting timing.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50592667,
          "author": "trueno",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T19:20:20.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50592667",
          "textZhHtml": "i would imagine the IPO has everything to do with it"
        },
        {
          "id": 50588118,
          "author": "hardbass",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T11:25:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50588118",
          "textZhHtml": "Apparently a mostly human/human only unfinished proof on the hypothesis was rushed to publication upon hearing this news. It is around 1000 pages long. I don't know what the approximate ratio should be but fully formal propfs generally are much longer than natural language proofs. So probably this size is ij 5he ballpark of normal. If anyone sho writes lean proofs or works in formalized mathematics can comment that would be nice."
        },
        {
          "id": 50588301,
          "author": "pfdietz",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T11:55:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50588301",
          "textZhHtml": "I am very skeptical of that human-only proof."
        },
        {
          "id": 50586205,
          "author": "begueradj",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T05:22:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50586205",
          "textZhHtml": "It more than 250 000 pages."
        },
        {
          "id": 50581274,
          "author": "CrimsonCape",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T20:13:06.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50581274",
          "textZhHtml": "I am a hobbyist home-server-builder and would love to know more about you and your company. I love hearing cases where you as an employee get direct access to the hardware. can you share any more details? I'm kind of spazzing out right now with how casually you said some wildly awesome stuff."
        },
        {
          "id": 50585636,
          "author": "hafeoz",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T03:32:32.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50585636",
          "textZhHtml": "I probably shouldn't say this, but I work at a company related to CPU manufacturing.<p>Part of my job is to ensure that HPC programs (largely LLM libraries these days) work on their next-generation CPUs and with new BIOS revisions. We test them using a shared pool of machines with various configurations. GPU machines are much scarcer, and we have to share them with other teams, so it's hard to reserve a time slot. I usually reserve a CPU machine around the clock so I can work whenever I want (I'm more productive at midnight), and nobody seems to mind.</p><p>I've worked here for almost a year now, and I love my company ;)</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50587719,
          "author": "freakynit",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T10:05:50.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50587719",
          "textZhHtml": "Intel? <a href=\"https://api.github.com/users/htzo\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://api.github.com/users/htzo</a>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50592781,
          "author": "NetMageSCW",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T19:30:26.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50592781",
          "textZhHtml": "“related”"
        },
        {
          "id": 50588161,
          "author": "whizzter",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T11:29:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50588161",
          "textZhHtml": "480 core xeon kinda hinted at that :D"
        },
        {
          "id": 50586978,
          "author": "lionkor",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T07:49:23.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50586978",
          "textZhHtml": "Thanks for sharing!"
        },
        {
          "id": 50585937,
          "author": "taneq",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T04:26:53.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50585937",
          "textZhHtml": "Hmm, an unnamed CPU manufacturer using a ton of a specifically named processor. No idea where you could be working, I’m stumped. ;)<p>Well I guess you did say ‘related to’ manufacturing which widens it a little. :P</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50574968.html",
