    },
    {
      "id": 50615308,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50615308",
      "rank": 4,
      "title": "\"Dario is hyping the dangers so OSS models are illegal and he stays in control\"",
      "titleZh": "\"Dario is hyping the dangers so OSS models are illegal and he stays in control\"",
      "url": "https://twitter.com/ylecun/status/2124550037912920090",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50615308",
      "author": "MrBuddyCasino",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T17:07:26.000Z",
      "score": 1834,
      "commentsCount": 1063,
      "bestRank": 3,
      "bestRankSource": "beststories",
      "maxScore": 1834,
      "maxComments": 1063,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-11-24T03:27:22.318Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-11-24T14:14:05.585Z",
      "appearances": 4,
      "category": "AI",
      "summaryZh": [
        "PostPostYann LeCun on X: \"Dario's spiel since GPT-2 has always been about hyping the dangers so that open models would be made illegal and *he* would stay in control. He left OpenAI because he wasn't going to have control. It's like the Ottoman Empire banning the printing press for Arabic so they would keep in control of the dogma. It stops the population from freely accessing diverse knowledge. It's a new form of medieval obscurantism.\"Conversation"
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50625796,
          "author": "kikdij",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T09:28:59.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50625796",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; The widespread use of robust non-recovery encryption ultimately will devastate our ability to fight crime and terrorism.<p>-- Louis Freeh, FBI director, in front of the senate, January 28th, 1998</p><p>That was when he tried to outlaw open-source encryption. We're here all over again. What certainly does not change is how a lot of people are scared of users freedom.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626534,
          "author": "tzfld",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:07:52.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626534",
          "textZhHtml": "Still seems a valid point. the question is how much freedom we are willing to give up for the ability to fight crime and terrorism."
        },
        {
          "id": 50626684,
          "author": "DrScientist",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:28:39.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626684",
          "textZhHtml": "Why? I'd contend it is based on a false premise - that secure digital comms is at the heart of crime and terrorism.<p>If I look at domestic terrorism events in the UK - most appear to have been done by individuals inspired by an idea rather than being part of some secret organisation held together by encrypted comms.</p><p>Likewise crime is either out of desperation, haphazard or done by people with severe problems.</p><p>Organised crime on the other hand requires both trust and fear, the sort that uses in person meetings to build and maintain.</p><p>I'd turn the 'if you have nothing to hide, why worry' argument on it's head.</p><p>If you have a reason to worry, why use digital encrption comms - when you can just meet in person or use very simple pre-agreed code phrases etc?</p><p>I accept that you can use mass surveillance to pick up suspicious behaviour of those lone terrorists ( internet history, purchase history etc ) - however such a tool in the wrong hands is much more dangerous than a few individuals in my view.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50628140,
          "author": "modo_mario",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T13:48:52.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50628140",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;most appear to have been done by individuals inspired by an idea rather than being part of some secret organisation held together by encrypted comms.<p>What's more is that those that are part of some secretish organisation often still just did their thing and had their mess upended by mass surveilance and backdoors only after they comitted their heinous deeds.</p><p>I feel like for the few that are blocked by this mess many many more are harmed or hampered and it would've been easier to not let the government forment the conditions for unrest or to allow many of those radical ideas to migrate into our societies with government sanction in the first place. It's not like it was popular.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50632794,
          "author": "rubberstamp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T18:58:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632794",
          "textZhHtml": "Now read it with the word \"government\" replaced by \"a particular group of people\". If they can look into us, why shouldn't we look into them? If they can, why we can't?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50630284,
          "author": "jdietrich",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T16:11:24.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50630284",
          "textZhHtml": "The most successful police operation against organised crime in British history came about as a result of compromising an encrypted chat network. Pretending that end-to-end encryption isn't massively useful to criminals is just silly - a more serious argument would be that the benefits outweigh the harms, or that the right to privacy is absolute.<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EncroChat\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EncroChat</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50630571,
          "author": "bad_haircut72",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T16:30:48.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50630571",
          "textZhHtml": "The fact the broke it open is just proof that police have other tools at their disposal like surveilance, informants and good old fashioned investigation that they dont need to be able to peer into everyones private lives."
        },
        {
          "id": 50626901,
          "author": "hadi77ir",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:56:00.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626901",
          "textZhHtml": "If one could communicate through \"blind, time-based communications\", should we ban time too? :D"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626576,
          "author": "ndr",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:13:37.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626576",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Those who would give up essential Liberty, to purchase a little temporary Safety, deserve neither Liberty nor Safety.<p>Definitely more prescient than he could have thought from any concerns he might have had at the time.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626688,
          "author": "gentooflux",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:28:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626688",
          "textZhHtml": "You can give up all the freedom you want, you have no right to give away mine."
        },
        {
          "id": 50630738,
          "author": "Anvoker",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T16:41:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50630738",
          "textZhHtml": "The way we keep, intentionally or unintentionally framing the problem as a simple tradeoff to be rationally investigated warps the whole discourse. I do not accuse you of doing anything wrong, but this seemingly neutral framing is not neutral at all in practice.<p>When you frame things this way people have a strong tendency to give up more and more for safety as long as they are not obviously and immediately affected (or when the obviously and immediately affected population is a minority).</p><p>It's death by a thousand cuts to liberties that end up shaping society in a deep manner.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626583,
          "author": "decide1000",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:14:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626583",
          "textZhHtml": "Zero. Banning books fails. Banning LLMs will fail as well.<p>Why is a commercial company creating these statements?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50629098,
          "author": "mrngld",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T14:53:26.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50629098",
          "textZhHtml": "I agree with you about banning things. But if you're talking about Dario, etc., he's got a right to his own opinion, and companies are simply groups of people. That you've got Nvidia and Anthropic, Jensen the White and Dario the Dark, saying opposite things is perfectly fine and should be viewed as healthy.<p>What wouldn't be healthy is if one of the aligned groups could silence others. Or if anyone took these companies opinions for more than what they are, since obviously they have varied interests (including making money). But that's true of individuals opinions as well, everyone has mixed motives. Part of being an adult is discernment.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50632834,
          "author": "rubberstamp",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T19:00:41.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632834",
          "textZhHtml": "But he doesn't have a right to do false advertisement in order to make profit. And the statements they make are basically advertisements that gets spread via news."
        },
        {
          "id": 50630094,
          "author": "reasonableklout",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T15:59:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50630094",
          "textZhHtml": "I mean, Dario himself says a ban is a bad idea here: <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/position-open-weights-models\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.anthropic.com/news/position-open-weights-models</a><p>I’m really confused at this whole thread to be honest. Seems a lot of people fear their models being taken away and Lecun is seizing on that</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626887,
          "author": "hadi77ir",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:54:08.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626887",
          "textZhHtml": "It's more like banning people from thinking, the right to own paper and more. What if people were able to think about \"dangerous\" things with their minds?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50628445,
          "author": "demo4567",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T14:10:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50628445",
          "textZhHtml": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_number\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_number</a><p>They will ban numbers if they are allowed by us. Dario is an authoritarian moron</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50630418,
          "author": "archimedes237",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T16:19:30.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50630418",
          "textZhHtml": "A person cant write a dangerous book before first having the dangerous thought, so, yes, we need to monitor thoughts with neuralink-like devices. If you have no thoughts to hide, then no one should object."
        },
        {
          "id": 50636812,
          "author": "adjejmxbdjdn",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T00:20:04.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50636812",
          "textZhHtml": "You can’t have nuclear proliferation without people. And you can’t have people without an earth. So we should blow up the earth!<p>Or…instead of making a silly slippery slope argument, we can cooperatively regulare nuclear proliferation and greatly minimize those risks without causing any or much harm.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50615308.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-11-24-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50614249,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50614249",
      "rank": 5,
      "title": "Ladybird is now in alpha",
      "titleZh": "Ladybird is now in alpha",
      "url": "https://ladybird.org/posts/alpha/",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50614249",
      "author": "janvdberg",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T16:13:12.000Z",
      "score": 2166,
      "commentsCount": 524,
      "bestRank": 2,
      "bestRankSource": "topstories",
      "maxScore": 2166,
      "maxComments": 524,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-11-23T16:27:28.112Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-11-24T14:14:05.585Z",
      "appearances": 6,
      "category": "科技产品",
      "summaryZh": [
        "Hello friends! Ladybird is now in alpha. You can download it for macOS and Linux. I’ve been looking forward to writing this post for a long time. :^) Ladybird is a browser with a new engine. The engine is our own, built from the ground up, and not a fork. The browser around it is modest, but functional. This is an alpha, so some websites won’t work, and it will crash sometimes. When that happens, tell us! You can report a broken site from inside the browser. After a crash, you can read the crash report and decide whether to send it. Our privacy policy has the details. We want security nerds to hack it! A new browser gets hacked, over and over, and that’s part of the process, so we make no promises about security today. When you pwn us, please tell us how so we can harden Ladybird! The alpha is a rolling channel, and we’ll be pushing out automatic updates regularly. This is the part I’m most excited about. Ladybird improves quickly, and until now you had to build it from source to see that. If you build websites, try yours in Ladybird and tell us what breaks. It supports WebDriver for automated testing, as well as the Firefox DevTools protocol. Thank you to our sponsors and donors, and to everyone who has tested, reported bugs, and written code over the years. There’s still a mountain of work ahead of us, but I’m very happy that you can finally try Ladybird for yourself!"
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50615429,
          "author": "freakynit",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T17:13:34.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50615429",
          "textZhHtml": "This actually worked amazingly well.<p>I tested a lot of sites.. a few that were already opened in my firefox, and a few many others.. Every single one of them rendered correctly.</p><p>This is truly amazing.. a new browser written from scratch that actually works!</p><p>The only minor issue: page scrolling is a bit jerky.. not much.. just a little bit.. it's noticeable.</p><p>A huge lots of congratulations to the Ladybird team. What a wonderful accomplishment truly this is.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50617209,
          "author": "kamranjon",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T19:01:17.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50617209",
          "textZhHtml": "Also mine is sipping on 53mb of memory with multiple tabs open - pretty awesome. I hope it avoids eventually turning into the type of experience I have with chrome and firefox where the browser ends up consuming 20gb of memory and has to be restarted periodically so I don't run out of system memory."
        },
        {
          "id": 50619963,
          "author": "simscitizen",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T21:55:53.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50619963",
          "textZhHtml": "Browsers are runtimes and webpages are apps. They just happen to be apps that are dynamically fetched over the network and are packaged as HTML/CSS/JS instead of as PE/ELF/Mach-O binaries.<p>Runtimes can have bugs or inefficiencies that cause them to use more memory than strictly necessary, but by and large most memory problems are the product of the apps (or webpages) themselves.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50624880,
          "author": "panta",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T07:33:55.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50624880",
          "textZhHtml": "The view of web pages as apps and browsers as runtime environments/OSes is reasonable but in my opinion it even reinforces the idea that it should be the browser duty to contain bad behavior from web apps/pages. Browsers are well positioned to do so, as they know exactly which resources are used by each tab , and how those grow over time. Also browsers already treat web content as untrusted (sandboxing, isolation), so it would be a natural next step to extend that approach to resource usage. It would match how Operating Systems work, where it's the OS acting as an \"arbiter\", not the userland application."
        },
        {
          "id": 50625357,
          "author": "paulluuk",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T08:28:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50625357",
          "textZhHtml": "I would love it if all the major browsers would agree on this. \"Every tab is only allowed up to 100mb of memory, if a page needs more than that, optimize it.\" - Though a setting from the user to allow it for, say, online videogames or streaming sites or something, would be a good idea."
        },
        {
          "id": 50629593,
          "author": "Wowfunhappy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T15:26:36.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50629593",
          "textZhHtml": "I remember when I was a Safari user, any instance of Slack I had open would get reloaded after 5 – 10 minutes with the message \"this web page was reloaded because it was using two much memory\", interrupting whatever I was doing.<p>My computer had many gigabytes of memory, so I always found this very annoying. Like, yes, Slack is a giant memory hog and that really sucks, but I can't exactly do anything about that.</p><p>Maybe Google would have the clout to actually enforce a memory limit like this, but I'm not sure whether anyone else does at this point. Apparently even Apple wasn't big enough.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50625442,
          "author": "copirate",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T08:40:04.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50625442",
          "textZhHtml": "Using more RAM could be a permission request."
        },
        {
          "id": 50625884,
          "author": "YmiYugy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T09:39:22.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50625884",
          "textZhHtml": "1. It would break a very large number of existing sites.\n2. The vast majority of users aren’t able to make an informed decision. \n3. Browsers already have heuristics to inform users about misbehaving tabs and suspend them.\n4. It hurts performance. More pressure on gc, more cache thrashing"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626734,
          "author": "brigandish",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:35:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626734",
          "textZhHtml": "It’s all about the UI. The browser vendor could make the informed guess and say “we think this web page is using too many resources”. Certainly Google could ascertain this during its spidering. Browsers already have several similar interventions e.g. no https, script taking too long etc.<p>Regardless, if it improved the experience then it becomes like Firefox was in the beginning, and Chrome in the beginning, a better performing competitor about to eat market share.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626057,
          "author": "throwaway2037",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T10:02:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626057",
          "textZhHtml": "Why do you think none of the major browsers have already done this?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626874,
          "author": "lo9i",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:52:38.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626874",
          "textZhHtml": "Agree, most people just want to visit a site and use it. Don't want to be bother with permission requests. You read the permission request the first 2, 3 times. Then just allow forever and you are back to the same scenario"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626104,
          "author": "dotancohen",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T10:08:08.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626104",
          "textZhHtml": "Because users would have no idea what to do.<p>I think the Corvette once had a special key for driving with enhanced performance, or maybe the key had to be turned to another position, I don't remember which. That caused so many service calls that later 'Vettes dropped it. And in the case of the Corvette, we're already talking about an enthusiast community that ostensibly actually takes an interest. Not so with people who call their browser \"the internet\".</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50627130,
          "author": "lproven",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T12:20:05.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50627130",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Because users would have no idea what to do.<p>Agreed. Nice idea but it's too much.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50625355,
          "author": "lukan",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T08:28:37.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50625355",
          "textZhHtml": "It is non trivial to determine if ressouces a site is using is reasonable or not. A web game may use lots of ressources and will crash if the OS suddenly decides to take some memory away."
        },
        {
          "id": 50623954,
          "author": "rileymat2",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T05:03:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50623954",
          "textZhHtml": "I am not following the logic.<p>Say I write a static html page that loads a series of images down the page where one image will fit on your screen at any time. If the browser loads them all into memory at once without releasing the memory or maintains the a fully rendered virtual page out of sight, is that bloat the app or runtime fault?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50624207,
          "author": "8n4vidtmkvmk",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T05:38:33.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50624207",
          "textZhHtml": "Both.<p>Runtime can certainly be optimized, but the app can also virtualize or force unloading if it needs to.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50631631,
          "author": "rileymat2",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T17:41:55.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50631631",
          "textZhHtml": "I am just talking about a page that is something like a descriptive document for the browser to deal with, probably less than 500 bytes.<p></p><pre><code>     &lt;html&gt;\n     &lt;body&gt;\n     &lt;img...&gt;\n     &lt;br&gt;\n     &lt;img..&gt;\n      ...\n      &lt;/body&gt;\n     &lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50635561,
          "author": "hdjrudni",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T22:14:47.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50635561",
          "textZhHtml": "Then the browser/runtime should optimize that as much as possible.<p>I suppose, sure, static webpages are closer to \"documents\" than \"apps\" and they should work well 'out of the box' without adding heaps of JS to optimize the browser's difficiencies.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50624589,
          "author": "mkgiga",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T06:47:50.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50624589",
          "textZhHtml": "It's a difficult question because there are so many factors in dom/css that change the answer to 'is there a chance that the image should be rendered the next frame?' that it becomes really hard to implement a strategy for what content to offload to disk and when<p>If the runtime fails to load the offloaded data back in time there will be visible rendering artifacts so you really have to make sure your strategy is robust</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50624658,
          "author": "imp0cat",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T06:59:07.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50624658",
          "textZhHtml": "If the static page is badly written and does not instruct the browser to use lazy loading (using the   loading=\"lazy\" attribute on the img tag) and the browser loads them all at the same time, is that a problem with the page or the browser?"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50614249.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-11-24-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50589618,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50589618",
      "rank": 6,
      "title": "Lean verification of the Riemann Hypothesis",
      "titleZh": "Lean verification of the Riemann Hypothesis",
      "url": "https://leanprover-community.github.io/blog/posts/riemann-hypothesis/",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50589618",
      "author": "g-b-r",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T14:36:45.000Z",
      "score": 1245,
      "commentsCount": 1052,
      "bestRank": 59,
      "bestRankSource": "topstories",
      "maxScore": 1245,
      "maxComments": 1052,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-11-23T16:27:28.112Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-11-24T14:14:05.585Z",
      "appearances": 3,
      "category": "开发工具",
      "summaryZh": [
        "Lean verification of the Riemann Hypothesis Joint statement by the Lean FRO and the Mathlib Initiative. On November 20, 2026, Levent Alpöge released a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis as a Lean library comprising 17 million lines of code. We have full confidence that Alpöge's proof of the Riemann Hypothesis is correct, and we congratulate him on this achievement. Our rationale is outlined below. The codebase depends directly only on Mathlib. Over the past day, the Lean and Mathlib communities have rallied to verify the proof's correctness. We hereby certify that both the statement and the proof of the Riemann Hypothesis are correct. The four criteria in https://xenaproject.wordpress.com/2026/11/12/and-the-proof-is-checked-in-lean/ are all satisfied.",
        "The statement of the Riemann Hypothesis is correct. This statement is part of Mathlib: RiemannHypothesis and has been carefully vetted by experts in Lean and number theory."
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50590943,
          "author": "rcr-anti",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T16:40:06.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50590943",
          "textZhHtml": "While still possible, the proof exploiting a bug is vanishingly small now. Following a few bugs and bad proofs this year, there are a number of different implementations, in different languages, and with different levels of proofs of their consistency. con-leche and con-ron are the heavy hitters, with consistency proofs of themselves. So for RH to be false, there'd have to be a bug in all the kernels that affects both the consistency proofs and the RH proof itself."
        },
        {
          "id": 50592163,
          "author": "magicalhippo",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T18:36:29.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50592163",
          "textZhHtml": "Since I'm not terribly familiar with Lean, math folks are generally satisfied the provided proof encodes the RH correctly I presume?<p>edit: I need to learn to read, that's the first part of their statement.</p><p><i>The statement of the Riemann Hypothesis is correct. This statement is part of Mathlib: RiemannHypothesis and has been carefully vetted by experts in Lean and number theory.</i></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50592224,
          "author": "brooke2k",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T18:42:32.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50592224",
          "textZhHtml": "The statement of RH that it proves is taken directly from Mathlib, so it's extremely well-vetted and longstanding (from my understanding)."
        },
        {
          "id": 50594571,
          "author": "jychang",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T23:11:14.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50594571",
          "textZhHtml": "Correct.<p>They don't use some random definition of RiemannHypothesis, they use the version here: <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/RiemannZeta.html#RiemannHypothesis\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib...</a></p><p>Note that this definition is years old. It's very well vetted. It's not a case of \"the proof works, it just doesn't actually prove what you think it proves\".</p><p>If you read the blog post, it says they added a \"Challenge.lean\" file. What that means is that instead of taking for granted the proof is trying to prove Riemann, they added a small file which reads RiemannHypothesis from the Mathlib library mentioned above, and forces the proof to match that.</p><p>They also mention running comparator, which is important because it checks for all the ways a proof might mathematically \"cheat\". For example, if you are trying to prove 1+2=5, and you redefine 2 as 4, then your bad proof would actually compile in Lean at first, but then comparator checks would discover all the tricks like that and declare the proof invalid.</p><p>Additionally, they mention compiling the proof with different Lean kernels, not just the mainline one. This rules out a way a proof This means the proof cannot just take advantage of a bug in the mainline Lean kernel. Importantly, they mention compiling the proof with con-leche, which uses a kernel that has been mathematically verified. Using con-leche is a lot slower, but the fact that the proof works on multiple kernels including con-leche means that it's not taking advantage of some bug in Lean.</p><p>At this point, I would say the proof is almost certainly true. The only obvious way it could be false is if ZFC itself is inconsistent or something wild like that, and the proof took advantage of it. But if ZFC is inconsistent, all of mathematics is in trouble.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50595497,
          "author": "intuitionist",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T02:01:03.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50595497",
          "textZhHtml": "Isn’t Lean stronger than ZFC? I thought you could prove Con(ZFC) within Lean. So it’s possible that the formal system encoded by Lean is inconsistent but ZFC is not. (I don’t consider this that likely, though.)"
        },
        {
          "id": 50596292,
          "author": "btilly",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T04:37:07.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596292",
          "textZhHtml": "Yes, Lean is stronger than ZFC. But two of the checkers, con=leche and con-ron, don't just verify based on Lean. They come with a formal proof from set theory that they cannot wind up accepting an incorrect proof.<p>In other words you can ignore Lean. We have a proof from ZFC, of the Riemann Hypothesis. We have a proof, acceptable in ZFC, that this proof is correct.</p><p>In other words Lean's dependent type theory is used to arrange its understanding of the proof. But the actual reasoning that you wind up with for this proof does not need anything that doesn't follow from ZFC.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50596244,
          "author": "tkz1312",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T04:29:02.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596244",
          "textZhHtml": "Yes, Lean is stronger than ZFC. lean has been shown to be consistent with ZFC + infinitely many inaccessible cardinals."
        },
        {
          "id": 50600534,
          "author": "cowlevel",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T15:26:32.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50600534",
          "textZhHtml": "isn't ZFC also consistent with ZFC + infinitely many inaccessible cardinals (I assume so or you wouldn't have even said it) which means by the same reasoning ZFC is stronger than ZFC?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50601966,
          "author": "tkz1312",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T17:45:13.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50601966",
          "textZhHtml": "no. if ZFC + infinitely many inaccessible cardinals is consistent, then so is ZFC (and lean), but the converse is not true: the consistency of ZFC implies nothing about the consistency of ZFC + infinitely many inaccessible cardinals."
        },
        {
          "id": 50598645,
          "author": "meowface",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T11:30:32.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50598645",
          "textZhHtml": "I wonder if in N years, when we (might) have crazy ASIs that make Fable 5.2 look like a nematode, maybe we'll see things like this as completely trivial and banal.<p>A machine intelligence might devise something as (seemingly) difficult as the Riemann hypothesis, but checking if what it posits is correct might be like a fast test command in a git repo, and so it can keep iterating on many ideas without being bottlenecked by waiting centuries or, perhaps even hours, to know if the ideas are true.</p><p>But then one could even imagine they'll one day be so powerful they could do this and more without ever using a formal system. They will just naturally be that comprehensive and rigorous internally, with something like Lean making them 100% instead of 99.99..% confident.</p><p>And eventually this would extend to almost everything, besides maybe biology where it may only be able to compress a human century into a day rather than into a second.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50601102,
          "author": "axmebhe",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T16:30:55.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50601102",
          "textZhHtml": "Mathematics could stall in some ways if the AIs continue to use brute force to prove these. Normally a mathematician would invent powerful new frameworks to solve a difficult problem (like Newton and calculus - there are many recent examples, as well).<p>As an outsider to the field, the strongest motivation for top mathematicians seems to be pride. I think the educational pipeline for top-level math will weaken if AIs are getting the credit for mathematical work.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50600406,
          "author": "idiotsecant",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T15:12:35.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50600406",
          "textZhHtml": "I think caring about things like the riemann hypothesis is something humans from the before times did. Once we have ASI, there is no incentive to push the bounds of knowledge, or even to try.<p>I think we're looking at the long dark teatime of the soul of biological human science, mathematics, and engineering.</p><p>I guess we will maybe be happier making art and being pets, best case scenario. Or maybe those of us with the most resources will be able to merge into a machine substrate and continue to contribute in some way.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50600731,
          "author": "thesmtsolver2",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T15:48:16.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50600731",
          "textZhHtml": "Why? With neural implants that these god like AIs can design worn we also become smart as the AIs?<p>Or do we believe that the AI will never get to a point where it can build implants that make humans that intelligent?</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50600793,
          "author": "LoganDark",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T15:54:40.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50600793",
          "textZhHtml": "I think it's because you no longer need to release knowledge if everyone can re-derive it independently. Work and learning lose its value when anyone can reproduce it without you, and when they can do it more trivially even than searching for your version. We're starting to reach the point where certain types of projects are just pointless now, because anyone who wants it can make their own version even better tailored to themselves. This isn't absolutely true of course, but the general discourse is heading in this direction, and this is one central focus of many modern imposter syndromes.<p>So everyone left without these tools to themselves is left in the dust. There are no more publishings of the most advanced knowledge and learnings, because everyone is expected to re-derive it from the prerequisites. But there's so much to derive that you just can't do it all on your own. It's not as natural and effortless as a large language model working through the course of whatever may require the knowledge and deriving it all in a depth first manner.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50602887,
          "author": "mrandish",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T19:14:21.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50602887",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; become smart as the AIs<p>Treating \"intelligence\" as a single trait that can be cranked up like an RPG stat bar is a category error built on an ill-defined term.</p><p>\"Intelligence\" is a broad umbrella label for a variety of different capabilities: working memory, pattern matching, sensory integration, long-term recall, and domain-specific knowledge. LLMs running on designed digital silicon are fundamentally different from human minds running on evolved analog biology. They operate on separate substrates with divergent architectures, trade-offs, constraints and failure modes.</p><p>LLMs are fantastically useful tools that already beat humans at specific tasks. We built them to simulate specific human intellectual output we find valuable, but they do so using mechanisms which have almost nothing in common with how a brain works. Scaling up the simulation to increase the number and depth of tasks doesn't mean the underlying process is on a path to fully replace human cognition and reasoning.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50596488,
          "author": "hazenut",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T05:09:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596488",
          "textZhHtml": "why is the verification is so fast this time while last time (a few weeks ago? and I can't remember name of that theorem) it took a few weeks and they finally found an error in Claude's proof?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50597828,
          "author": "mcherm",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T09:36:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597828",
          "textZhHtml": "I am not certain which \"last time\" you are thinking of, but I suspect that the answer IS the Lean verification.<p>If an AI writes a paper with an argument that X is true, it might be that X is true, <i>or</i> it might be that the argument in the paper is subtly flawed and X isn't true</p><p>On the other hand, if an AI generates a step-by-step proof in which each step can be mechanically verified, then people might have problems understanding the underlying ideas, but once it has been verified we know it won't have subtle errors. And that is basically what Lean is.</p><p>In this case, they not only have a Lean proof, they've verified it with more than one verification tool (so it's unlikely to be a bug in the verification tool) and double-checked whether the thing being proved really is what we intended to prove. That's rock solid.</p><p>Getting an UNDERSTANDING of the proof is another challenge, but at this point we can be quite certain it doesn't have errors.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50601276,
          "author": "hazenut",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T16:44:36.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50601276",
          "textZhHtml": "Navier-Stokes that is. Claimed proof in September, found an error in October, mainly that the formal language proof does not correspond to the natural language description. But this time it took one day. that's 30x speedup not explained."
        },
        {
          "id": 50599758,
          "author": "BariumBlue",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T13:56:47.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50599758",
          "textZhHtml": "This time the WHOLE proof formalized in lean, but iirc some recent AI generated proofs only had supplemental pieces lean that didn't cover the whole argument"
        },
        {
          "id": 50597849,
          "author": "gus_massa",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T09:40:00.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597849",
          "textZhHtml": "The guy waited until he has the verification before anouncing it. I'll quote myself from the pararell universe were he announced it with a English only proof:<p>&gt; <i>Meh. Lean or it didn't happen.</i></p>"
        }
      ],
