      "title": "Toyota, Nissan, and Honda to standardize parts to lower costs",
      "titleZh": "Toyota, Nissan, and Honda to standardize parts to lower costs",
      "url": "https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a74490092/toyota-nissan-honda-standardize-parts-lower-costs/",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596133",
      "author": "zdw",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T04:04:56.000Z",
      "score": 1388,
      "commentsCount": 733,
      "bestRank": 2,
      "bestRankSource": "beststories",
      "maxScore": 1388,
      "maxComments": 733,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-11-23T16:27:28.112Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-11-24T14:14:05.585Z",
      "appearances": 5,
      "category": "AI",
      "summaryZh": [
        "Toyota, Nissan, and Honda will begin standardizing some vehicle parts in an effort to lower production costs, according to a report by Automotive News. The automakers will start with equalized quality criteria for steel, consistent protocols for plastics, and a universal color-coding scheme for wiring harnesses.Discussions are starting with Japan’s domestic suppliers, though international suppliers will be looped in soon.Japan’s three largest automakers have agreed to begin standardizing vehicle parts in an effort to reduce costs, according to Automotive News. The outlet reports that Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association chairman Koji Sato, who is also the vice chairman of Toyota, announced the decision at a news conference in Tokyo this week.In the short term, the standardization efforts will begin with unified quality criteria for steel, consistent protocols for plastics and resins, and a universal color-coding scheme for wiring harnesses. In the long term, the Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association has plans to move to more components that cover the entire car. The three areas were chosen as the first to be standardized because they are parts that are generally not noticed by consumers, and they shouldn’t have an impact on any brand’s individual identity. All three are also areas that account for significant production costs for the automakers, meaning they can have an immediate impact.CFOTO/Future Publishing via Getty Images|Getty ImagesThe first discussions of standardization are set to include Japan’s domestic automotive parts suppliers, though international suppliers will be brought into the fold in time. The first standardization efforts are set to go into place next year, according to the AN report.The push to simplify and lower costs comes as Japan’s seven automakers, including Toyota, Nissan, Honda, Mazda, Subaru, Mitsubishi, and Suzuki, work to fend off the increasingly competitive list of Chinese automakers. The automakers are said to hope that they can channel the money saved by homogenizing into new innovations.“We all share this strong sense of crisis, creating a momentum for change,” Sato said. “Based upon our sense of a crisis, we decided to choose these three areas.”➡️ Skip the lot. Let Car and Driver help you find your next car.Jack Fitzgerald’s love for cars stems from his as yet unshakable addiction to Formula 1. After a brief stint as a detailer for a local dealership group in college, he knew he needed a more permanent way to drive all the new cars he couldn’t afford and decided to pursue a career in auto writing. By hounding his college professors at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee, he was able to travel Wisconsin seeking out stories in the auto world before landing his dream job at Car and Driver. His new goal is to delay the inevitable demise of his 2010 Volkswagen Golf."
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50596443,
          "author": "johnohara",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T05:02:40.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596443",
          "textZhHtml": "Ford and GM have been doing this since 2002 with the GM–Ford 6-speed automatic transmission [0]. The companies worked together on the design and ultimately worked with Allison Transmission on its manufacture.<p>One key difference between each company's implementation had to do with the shifting solenoids and the electronics. But as far as I know the actual clutch pads and gearing were the same.</p><p>[0]: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GM%E2%80%93Ford_6-speed_automatic_transmission\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GM%E2%80%93Ford_6-speed_automa...</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50596604,
          "author": "lelanthran",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T05:30:20.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596604",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Ford and GM have been doing this since 2002 with the GM–Ford 6-speed automatic transmission [0]<p><i>All</i> the manufacturers have been doing it since even before that, with major items like engines and transmissions.</p><p>What they <i>haven't</i> been doing is standardising all those other parts that need replacing. For example, standardising on the wheel hub+knuckle for 15-inch, 16-inch, 17-inch and 18-inch wheels will let wheels of the specific size use the same disks, calipers, pads, balljoint, wheel bearing, CV joint and all the sensors (wheel speed, brake, etc).</p><p>I see no reason why two different SUVs on 17-inch wheels need <i>completely</i> different brake pads shapes, when both sets have the same surface area, require the same performance profile, etc, but that's what we have now.</p><p>You can do the same with almost every mechanical and electrical subsystem in a car, and <i>still</i> have a large variety in cars produced. After all, car manufacturers don't manufacture their own tyres and batteries, do they? They don't manufacture they own wheels.</p><p>Even the things that are specific to them, they outsource anyway - they don't each have their own windscreen manufacturer, they outsource that. The already outsource all the glass, all the oils and fluids, all the pipes, etc, it's jus that they produce incompatible designs so that the actual parts manufacturer has to charge a jig setup fee for each of them. Standardising on parts brings in economies of scale.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50603298,
          "author": "r3trohack3r",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T19:58:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50603298",
          "textZhHtml": "To this point, I think an AutoZone and/or O’Reilly’s episode of Acquired would be incredible. The fact that I can drive down the street and get a random part for a 20 year old car is incredible. Sometimes walking out with it, sometimes from their distribution network.<p>They’re a Right to Repair aligned business model.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50610142,
          "author": "thelastgallon",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T11:08:29.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50610142",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; They’re a Right to Repair aligned business model.<p>We need right to repair (for many many good reasons), but this must be reframed and articulated to legacy car manufacturers as Commoditize your complement.</p><p>Legacy mfrs know (lets pretend) how to make cars, but they definitely don't know how to make batteries. It looks like we are headed towards a CATL and BYD duopoly. The first thing car manufacturers must do is standardize batteries and open source BMS and make batteries replaceable like batteries. There may not be current competition, but enable future competition. Eventually, some govts somewhere will start subsidizing locally sourced batteries, then your cars are future proofed and ready to swap in without changing design.</p><p>We can avoid the phone duopoly, if car manufacturers recognize their days are over, its all EVs, they still have some brand value (fwiw) and leverage that brand value with mostly commoditized components, pretty much everything except for the shell. These are batteries, all electrical components, heat pumps, sensors, electronics, etc. Make everything standardized just like wheels. And all manners of software should be open source: BMS, ADAS, self-driving, etc. And infotainment, instrument panel controls replacing carplay/android with a corresponding phone app (eventually evolving into a third phone ecosystem).</p><p>Legacy mfrs have learnt by now that they don't know how to make batteries. What Happened to America's Giant EV Battery Factories: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Jq9MADu8bA\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Jq9MADu8bA</a></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50612003,
          "author": "dzonga",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T14:22:15.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50612003",
          "textZhHtml": "my take legacy manufacturers from Japan slept on the job.<p>the world is going electric. but they had everything to capture what most of the world needs in Extended Range Electric Vehicles - which are more practical than pure EVs.</p><p>they know how to make small engines (Honda, Yamaha), they know how to make hybrid powertrains (toyota) - they just needed to put out decent batteries and electric motors and not waffle about.</p><p>they can still capture that market since Chinese EREVs are not yet that good - compared to their EVs.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50613028,
          "author": "rasz",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T15:05:32.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50613028",
          "textZhHtml": "Japanese car companies were one of the firsts with EV prototypes all the way in the seventies! under 1971 MITI/AIST program<p><a href=\"https://japanesenostalgiccar.com/japanese-automakers-ev-concepts-1973-tokyo-motor-show/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://japanesenostalgiccar.com/japanese-automakers-ev-conc...</a></p><p>But going Hydrogen became a Political decision somewhere in late nineties.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50610094,
          "author": "atillaisik",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T11:02:11.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50610094",
          "textZhHtml": "I really love and support that but the new manifacture cars design to only open up the hood at their service only and if you try to open up the car engine by your self they try to gt it out of warenty"
        },
        {
          "id": 50629406,
          "author": "mech998877",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T15:15:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50629406",
          "textZhHtml": "As a mechanical engineer this comment strikes me as too optimistic and borderline naive.<p>There are hundreds of reasons why different steering components have different shapes. By the time you are down to designing the steering knuckles and wheel hubs of cars, you are already working with vehicle weight, geometry constraints (which are a big deal), backward compatibility to simplify supply chain, material availability, outside suppliers, manufacturability, steering ratios, existing tooling, trying to minimize unsprung weight, servicability, whether the car uses a mcpherson strut or some other suspension setup, FWD vs AWD, etc, etc, etc.</p><p>It is almost an overconstrained problem to design these parts for a single vehicle. The combinatorial explosion of how many different ways there are to design the part is worse than you think it is.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50597069,
          "author": "ExoticPearTree",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T07:12:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597069",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; I see no reason why two different SUVs on 17-inch wheels need completely different brake pads shapes, when both sets have the same surface area, require the same performance profile, etc, but that's what we have now.<p>Because an SUV that has 17\" wheels can weigh two tons or three tons or more. It can do 120 mph maximum speed or it can go to 160 mph or more.</p><p>The math changes really fast when it comes to stopping that SUV down based on those two parameters alone. You can see why different calipers, how many, brake disk size, type (ventilated or not), different sized brake pads.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50597140,
          "author": "lelanthran",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T07:29:45.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597140",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt;&gt; I see no reason why two different SUVs on 17-inch wheels need completely different brake pads shapes, when both sets have the same surface area, require the same performance profile, etc, but that's what we have now.<p>&gt; Because an SUV that has 17\" wheels can weigh two tons or three tons or more. It can do 120 mph maximum speed or it can go to 160 mph or more.</p><p>Didn't I address this objection in advance with the words \"performance profile\"?</p><p>Right now, 95% of SUVs have the same brake performance requirements: under 4 ton, under 160mph, etc. There is no cost saving in making an entirely different wheel assembly for SUVs that require under 3.99 tons and under 159mph, which is what the current manufacturers are doing.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50605384,
          "author": "SkyPuncher",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T23:45:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50605384",
          "textZhHtml": "I don’t think this is true at all.  Towing and payload vary heavily across models, which is the most direct need to technical performance.<p>Different models are also targeting different driving characteristics. Higher performance pads bite more which might be undesirable for smooth, luxury vehicles. While those same pads and rotors would be problematic while towing.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50610222,
          "author": "lbriner",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T11:19:22.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50610222",
          "textZhHtml": "Not sure you are disagreeing though. Having different brake pads within a standard range would be normal enough, we are talking more about the private of high-cost items like the brake caliper itself.<p>For top-end vehicles, sure, they might want slightly more braking or slightly lighter components and will invest in that but for most cars, I suspect that more parts could be standardised.</p><p>Actually, a lot of parts are already standardised including wing mirrors, electronics and smaller engine components so it is possible to do but sure, it's scary giving up your engine design time and trusting that you can use the same engine as a rival but still have a USP for your customers.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50597365,
          "author": "ExoticPearTree",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T08:11:21.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597365",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; Didn't I address this objection in advance with the words \"performance profile\"?<p>I did not understood your statement like that.</p><p>I don’t know about cost savings, but most likely it is about design decisions regarding how a wheel should look like (brake calipers in front of the wheel, in the back).</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50597716,
          "author": "lelanthran",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T09:14:37.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50597716",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; most likely it is about design decisions regarding how a wheel should look like (brake calipers in front of the wheel, in the back).<p>It's irrelevant in 95% of cars. All of the things I specified are irrelevant in 95% of cars.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50606863,
          "author": "lazide",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T02:38:27.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50606863",
          "textZhHtml": "Because that is ludicrous. A suburban and a rav4 have not only have completely different performance profiles while nominally meeting your requirements - but try to be in different fleet fuel efficiency groupings. And the equipment for a suburban is definitely not going to help the rav4 meet it’s efficiency targets."
        },
        {
          "id": 50599499,
          "author": "cucumber3732842",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T13:26:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50599499",
          "textZhHtml": "Brakes got big and dirverse because \"muh handlig\" and \"muh moose test\" (and \"muh $500 replacement rim when you hit a curb) drive tire and rim size.  Big rims add weight.  So they make the brakes lighter by using a bigger diameter rotor and smaller/lighter caliper.  The move from traditional spindles and/or press in bearings to bolt on unit bearings hasn't helped either since you're carrying around a few pounds of extra mounting flange.<p>A big honking cast iron caliper grabbing 12\" rotor that mounts from the back of the hub (to reduce the hat diameter letting you have swept area right up to the wheel bolt circle) can really take some heat and the whole thing will package in a 16\" rim.  But it ain't gonna be light.  I get that we're gonna have different \"classes\" of performance and you won't put that brake assembly on a new land rover even if it has comparable demands to the old van but there is a whole lot of room to consolidate here.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50602696,
          "author": "numpad0",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T18:54:05.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50602696",
          "textZhHtml": "They can like idk, use exact same capiler mounting brackets or mounting screws or pre-cut brake hoses or things like that for vans and trucks, then fit differently sized pads or even the whole calipers each.<p>That will eliminate like, technically different washers and nuts used for screws to mount the truck calipers that goes this way versus van calipers that comes through this here using slightly longer screws, and that kinds of things add up.</p><p>A lot of comment heree seem to be fixated on metal parts, but I think it's more about plastics and wires and electronics. If you could use a common harneess for compacts and sedans and trucks going forward and backward and across... saves a lot of inventory cost.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50602885,
          "author": "SoftTalker",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T19:14:09.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50602885",
          "textZhHtml": "There already is some commonality within manufacturers. Ever look under the hood or dashboard and notice a connector on the wiring harness that's not connected to anything? That's because they used the same harness as another model that needs that connector. It's cheaper to design, produce, stock, and manage one harness even if there's extra wire and connectors that aren't used on every car. It probably doesn't happen across manufacturers though, but there are SAE standard connector types at least."
        },
        {
          "id": 50604155,
          "author": "limagnolia",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T21:35:35.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50604155",
          "textZhHtml": "It is always a trade off between standardizing and differentiating.  At some point, performance and technology converge, and it is no longer worth it to differentiate designs, thus you standardize.  I am no expert on what parts have reached that point, but there likely are a lot that have."
        },
        {
          "id": 50610195,
          "author": "lbriner",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T11:15:00.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50610195",
          "textZhHtml": "This!<p>We had this when the Peugeot 107, Citroen C1 and Toyota Aygo were basically the same car. For the manufacturers, there was a significant cost saving but ultimately you relied on brand loyalty to sell whichever of them was not the cheapest model since they varied but a not insignificant amount.</p><p>Of course, the other area where most manufacturers make their money is after-sales \"Service\" although since many dealers, at least in the UK, are independent, the manufacturer can't easily control the profitability of these. My wife uses a Hyundai dealer who are really poorly run. Hard to book the car in, really inconvenient \"leave it with us all day\" etc. so not sure that is a great way to make money in the current model.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50596133.html",
      "fullCommentsUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/hn/2026-11-24-full-comments.json"
    },
    {
      "id": 50583761,
      "storyKey": "hackernews-50583761",
      "rank": 9,
      "title": "Ask HN: I have been unemployed for 2y. 15 years of experience, US. What to do?",
      "titleZh": "Ask HN: I have been unemployed for 2y. 15 years of experience, US. What to do?",
      "url": "",
      "hnUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50583761",
      "author": "LetThingsBeDiff",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T23:18:32.000Z",
      "score": 737,
      "commentsCount": 836,
      "bestRank": 107,
      "bestRankSource": "topstories",
      "maxScore": 737,
      "maxComments": 836,
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-11-23T16:27:28.112Z",
      "lastSeenAt": "2026-11-23T16:27:28.112Z",
      "appearances": 1,
      "category": "AI",
      "summaryZh": [
        "I'm Senior software engineer in the US (DMV area), with 15y of experience. Worked for Fortune 50+ company and bunch of mid-size companies. Been unemployed for 2y and can't find a job, within those 2 years, I pretty much spent my savings that I've saved through"
      ],
      "highlightsZh": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 50593314,
          "author": "powvans",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T20:28:16.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50593314",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; In Jan 2025, I quit from a mid-size insurance tech company(1000+) after working there for almost 4y, because I was tipped off the they were about to start layoffs and they did. Well, since then I have zero luck finding any job in SWE.<p>I don't know the OP's circumstances. What I am about to say is meant as advice to others and not a criticism of the OP.</p><p>If you suspect that you may be the target of a layoff, DO NOT SIMPLY QUIT. In this scenario you are a potential liability to your employer. They will almost certainly pay you to leave if they decide to part ways.</p><p>It is always easier to find a job when you have a job. Maybe you think if there are layoffs and you are retained that it will be a miserable working environment. Fine. DO NOT QUIT. After a round of layoffs you are relatively safe and you can use that time to find another job.</p><p>It is very likely that layoffs are going to continue as companies restructure because of AI. Have a plan. Keep your wits and hold fast.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50599414,
          "author": "al_borland",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T13:16:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50599414",
          "textZhHtml": "There are also companies that do layoffs routinely. When I started where I work, they didn't. I remember the first time I experienced layoffs, it was a dark day in the office. These days, they happen at least twice per year, and that's been the trend for many years now. I've been at the company for over 2 decades now, and have watched countless rounds of layoffs happen while I retained my job. Never actually feeling like you have job security when being somewhere for 20 years is not fun, but it does highlight that they aren't always the end. On year, I was told 3 times I was getting laid off, I had meetings with HR and signed severance papers and everything... as I left the office my boss told me not to worry... that was 10 years ago. I'm still working there, that boss was laid off a few years later.<p>It could be that companies that historically didn't do layoffs are starting to move into the regular cadence I've been experiencing for years. To echo the parent, I don't know OP's situation, but these scenarios exist.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50636422,
          "author": "kbrisso",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T23:38:25.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50636422",
          "textZhHtml": "I call bullshit on the severance paper signing. I was at a company for 25 years and went through countless layoffs and out sourcing. Sooner or later your time will come mine did and I just had a great year end review. It's your age - it was mine - I got a great severance but I signed papers saying if you take the cash you can't sue. I never thought I was bulletproof, I kept me resume updated and my skill set up to date. I hope you don't get laid off but I'm just saying..."
        },
        {
          "id": 50606316,
          "author": "gmmachine",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T01:29:12.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50606316",
          "textZhHtml": "I was in a department that operated like that. This philosophy, of an ever present Damoclesian layoff, it comes from Jack Welch right?<p>Why hire someone in the first place just to lay them off? That's what we should ask as labor.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50608567,
          "author": "johnnyanmac",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T07:31:21.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50608567",
          "textZhHtml": "They lay off because it's easy money (stock prices traditionally raise after a layoff announcement), regulatory bodies don't punish it, and a variety of more personal power dynamics at play.<p>A company that truly only needs project-to-project transient talent would look for a contracting firm. But they do not do that partially out of tradition, and partially out of that power dynamic (you bully a contractor too much too consistently and other business people at that firm will get mad at you). But very little of this current structure is designed with employee satisfaction or even business efficiency in mind.</p><p>These are still people with personal ambitions beyond a mere paycheck; those wanting easy guaranteed money would simply invest more. Not run a business.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50626858,
          "author": "msdz",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-24T11:50:17.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626858",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; regulatory bodies don't punish it<p>This is why there's more to how livable a jurisdiction is than just raw GDP per capita… I couldn't imagine the type of job insecurity anxiety this behavior would wreak if it were legal to do layoffs in this manner over here.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50593736,
          "author": "mrbonner",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T21:17:29.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50593736",
          "textZhHtml": "I was reading that line and wondering the same thing. I know OP may feel pride and such (I would too)."
        },
        {
          "id": 50593809,
          "author": "vigilantpuma",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T21:27:10.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50593809",
          "textZhHtml": "Pride? Where is the shame in being laid off?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50593908,
          "author": "ChuckMcM",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T21:39:23.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50593908",
          "textZhHtml": "Well, in my experience that regardless of how its done, when the company asks you to leave and continues on without you, it feels like they fired you and that you were not \"picked\" to continue on with them. If the company dissolves after laying you off that is a different thing.<p>For employees its a double negative, new employers will see being laid off as \"you weren't part of the top 10%\" and by leaving on your own terms, \"this guy's a quitter, he might quit on us too.\" Which is really just people trying to find excuses to not hire. When I was on the hiring rotation at Google I asked the committee once \"Why do you bring these people in if you already don't think you will hire them?\" The whole \"Maybe they will surprise us...\" rings hollow.</p><p>My advice to the folks like the OP living in the US is to use your IT experience to help folks in Active Senior kinds of communities with their IT issues. You can do it for 'free' or a nominal charge. This is an easy way to network with successful former executives who still have contacts in the industry.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50594104,
          "author": "eloisant",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T22:06:19.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50594104",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; new employers will see being laid off as \"you weren't part of the top 10%\"<p>No that's not the case. There are many reasons your employer decide you're the one the be laid off that are not related to performances. Maybe your salary is higher. Maybe your whole department or location is shutdown. New employers know that and don't judge laid off people.</p><p>&gt; this guy's a quitter, he might quit on us too.</p><p>That can be true, but only if you have a short tenure in the companies you worked for. Companies might think that if you have multiple jobs with less than a year. But if you quit a company after working for at least 2/3 years, companies won't judge you for that.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50594165,
          "author": "Plasmoid",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T22:13:49.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50594165",
          "textZhHtml": "&gt; New employers know that and don't judge laid off people.<p>While nothing is universal, there are lots of companies that will absolutely not hire someone who has been laid off on the basis that they are the \"scraps\" or \"they were cut for a reason\".</p><p>It's awful but it's not exactly uncommon.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 50594222,
          "author": "tibbar",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T22:19:58.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50594222",
          "textZhHtml": "Yes. Unfortunately, in a previous job I saw candidates with stellar resumes, interview results, and references blocked from an offer because they were part of a layoff in the past. It was shocking to me at the time, but it definitely happens."
        },
        {
          "id": 50595764,
          "author": "cebert",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T02:57:57.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50595764",
          "textZhHtml": "There should be laws against this"
        },
        {
          "id": 50596708,
          "author": "Pokepokalypse",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T05:57:40.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596708",
          "textZhHtml": "Laws against having an opinion, even if it's wrong?\nHow do you even enforce that?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50602676,
          "author": "allarm",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T18:51:42.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50602676",
          "textZhHtml": "Several examples come to mind of opinions that are not viewed favorably by society or by the laws of several countries."
        },
        {
          "id": 50601363,
          "author": "vigilantpuma",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T16:52:43.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50601363",
          "textZhHtml": "You know there are already a bunch of things that you can’t use to discriminate against people in employment, right?"
        },
        {
          "id": 50596138,
          "author": "monksy",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T04:06:12.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596138",
          "textZhHtml": "But we all live in America. Where the government and private controllers don't want workers to be independent."
        },
        {
          "id": 50594347,
          "author": "smackeyacky",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T22:39:56.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50594347",
          "textZhHtml": "I would agree with this.  Seeing a new org chart without your name stings quite a bit"
        },
        {
          "id": 50595062,
          "author": "vigilantpuma",
          "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T00:36:07.000Z",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50595062",
          "textZhHtml": "I know it’s PAINFUL to beaid off. I have that experience firsthand (and while I was on maternity leave to boot)! But I was never ashamed to be laid off."
        }
      ],
      "detailUrl": "https://wshyhlm1.github.io/forum-digest/stories/hackernews-50583761.html",
